    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test run, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`, the sole place owner telephones are normalized.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **Added a country-code \u2192 national-length map** (`COUNTRY_NATIONAL_LENGTH`): `+1` (NANP) requires 10 national digits, `+61` (Australia) requires 9. Country codes not in the map are left un-length-checked so other international numbers stay accepted.\n\n2. **Extended `toE164(...)`** to determine the applicable country code:\n   - When the input has a `+` prefix, `matchCountryCode` picks the longest known country code the digits start with (or `null` if unknown).\n   - When there's no country code, `+61` is assumed (matching the existing default).\n   \n   After the existing 8\u201315 digit sanity check, if the country code is one with a known length and the national number (digits after the country code) doesn't match exactly, it throws `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` \u2192 HTTP 400 naming `telephone`.\n\n3. **Updated the Javadoc** on `normalizeTelephone`/`toE164` and imported `java.util.Comparator`.\n\nThe new acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (which posts `+61 123`) now correctly gets a 400, and the valid `+61`-with-9-national-digits path used by `uniqueTelephone()` (and every other acceptance rule) still succeeds.",
